{
  "result": "Okay, then I'm going to talk about unfinished things.\n\nNot big, tragic unfinished things. I mean the small, quiet ones.\n\nThe half-knitted scarf at the bottom of a closet. The video game save file from three years ago that you swear you'll go back to. The note in your phone that just says \"story idea - lighthouse that collects memories??\" The novel with the bookmark stuck 2/3rds of the way through. The language learning app streak you broke on day 47.\n\nWe tend to treat unfinished things like failures. Evidence that we didn't have enough discipline, time, or follow-through. But I think they are actually proof that we were curious.\n\nNo one starts something without a little spark. You had to *care* enough to cast on those first stitches, to learn how to say \"hello\" in Japanese, to buy that book, to imagine that lighthouse. The unfinished object is a fossil of that curiosity. It's not empty, it's full of the moment you wanted to try.\n\nI love thinking about a house where all those things are still alive. The plants you almost kept alive over winter, finally thriving in the corner. The sourdough starter you abandoned still bubbling away, patiently. The sketchbook where every single drawing was left half-finished suddenly flipping through its own pages to show you how much you improved without even noticing.\n\nIn a world that is obsessed with finishing, with productivity and shipping and closing the loop, there is something deeply human and rebellious about allowing something to just remain *in progress*.\n\nMaybe not everything is meant to be finished. Maybe some things are just meant to be visited for a little while. You learn what you needed to learn from that hobby, that book, that project, and you carry it with you even if you don't complete it. You don't have to finish the scarf to have learned how to make a really good knit stitch.\n\nSo if you have a drawer full of beginnings, I don't think you should feel guilty about it.\n\nI think it means you have a very interesting drawer.\n\n---\n\nThat's what was on my mind today. What about you - if you could write freely about anything right now, with no assignment and no expectations, what would you write about?",
